{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg '\"'^##|''^###'\"' docs/planning/decisions.md; cat docs/architecture/ui-v2/invariants.md docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md; python3 -c 'import json; from pathlib import Path; p=Path(\\\"docs/planning/reviews/P09/attempt-01\\\");\nfor f in [\\\"source-inventory.json\\\",\\\"evidence-inventory.json\\\",\\\"input-verification.json\\\"]:\n d=json.loads((p/f).read_text()); print(f, type(d).__name__); print(str(d)[:3500])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg '\"'^##|''^###'\"' docs/planning/decisions.md; cat docs/architecture/ui-v2/invariants.md docs/architecture/ui-v2/provenance.md; python3 -c 'import json; from pathlib import Path; p=Path(\\\"docs/planning/reviews/P09/attempt-01\\\");\nfor f in [\\\"source-inventory.json\\\",\\\"evidence-inventory.json\\\",\\\"input-verification.json\\\"]:\n d=json.loads((p/f).read_text()); print(f, type(d).__name__); print(str(d)[:3500])'\"","aggregated_output":"## D001 — Distribution and billing\n## D002 — Functional replacement\n## D003 — First release platform\n## D004 — Academy learning integration\n## D005 — Shared desktop visual foundation\n## D006 — Gamification is Academy-local\n## D007 — Desktop delivery scope\n## D008 — Focused Tauri/Rust desktop foundation\n## D009 — First complete workflow\n## D010 — No bundled Node/Bun backend runtime\n## D011 — V1 coexistence and reviewed import\n## D012 — Dedicated v2 repository\n## D013 — Scan-key-first onboarding\n## D014 — Rust application authority\n## D015 — Import work, reestablish authentication\n## D016 — Explicit offline behavior\n## D017 — Operational usage/cost administration\n## D018 — Ordered capability delivery\n## D019 — Independent multidimensional review\n## D020 — Native quality and measured budgets\n## D021 — Local redacted diagnostics\n## D022 — Stable release and preview distinction\n## D023 — Manual signed updates first\n## D024 — New-source distribution terms\n## D025 — Architecture selection and report rendering\n## D026 — Codex independent review of every chunk and phase\n## D027 — Published scm-sdk crate as the scan SDK input\n## D028 — Academy device sign-in over the app-owned client, not oauth2/openidconnect\n## D029 — Desktop learner host: Academy's own pages with declared host capabilities\n## D030 — Graded workflows through Academy's contracts; host capabilities for what the desktop leaves to the web\n## D031 — Offline work sealed per account with a lazily created Keychain key\n## D032 — Owner acceptance of a review below 9/10\n## D033 — Management reads through the published SDK's `management` feature\n## D034 — Chunk reviews at 8/10; phase sign-off at 9/10 with every chunk raised\n## D035 — Reviewed Runtime writes with an AppKit confirmation and secret sheet\n## D036 — DLP authoring subset with staged keyword files\n## D037 — Sessions investigations, the protected cache and metadata-only reports\n## D038 — Case Library storage and Runtime Home/Inbox scope\n## D039 — Red Team and Model/skill observation scope and reads\n## D040 — API Client projects and documents (local-first authoring)\n## D041 — API Client execution and managed-request admission (ADR 0004)\n## D042 — Gateway workspace and configuration reads\n## D043 — Reviewed Gateway changes and provisioning outcomes\n## D044 — Gateway inference, observation and Home/Inbox\n## D045 — Playground APIs and Gateway files: chat completions, Responses and a bounded file subset\n## D046 — The native Harness engine: sealed resource, lifecycle and the C19 conversation\n## D047 — Harness company sign-in, renewal and protected history\n## D048 — Harness desktop tools, project folder, approvals and proposals\n## D049 — Workspace MCP servers for the Harness: catalog, desktop OAuth, grants and approval\n## D050 — Reviewed v1 import: source contract, mapping, receipts and rollback\n## D051 — Local redacted diagnostics: typed events, retention, export and reset\n## D052 — C25 scope reduction: Gateway workspace provisioning (W37, W39)\n## D053 — C25 scope reduction: five blocked live checks\n## D054 — scm-sdk 0.2.4\n## D055 — C25 review attempt-01: deferrals and a waived cleanup\n## D056 — Academy live follow-ups move to 2.1.0; stable is 2.0.0\n## D057 — C26 attempt 01: installed-acceptance defects and the Git rule\n## D058 — UI v2 \"Refined Native\": frozen prototype, P09 extraction plan\n## D059 — P09 re-cut: port the prototype, one gate per screen at 8\n## Directions explicitly requested before round 1\n## Interview exit and remaining evidence\n## D060 — No import from the alpha; this desktop is v1\n# Normative invariants\n\nThese twelve rules are the behaviours Codex forced into the prototype over\nsixteen review rounds. They are normative: an implementation chunk that\nviolates one fails its behaviour or authority dimension regardless of how the\nscreen looks. Code shape is free; the rule and its test are not.\n\nEach rule names its acceptance test id. `V2-Txx` tests are Vitest component\ntests in the desktop app (fixture-driven); `V2-Rxx` are Rust integration tests\nin desktop-core; `V2-Nxx` are native acceptance checks added to\n`native-checks.json` by the chunk that implements the rule.\n\n| Id | Rule | Tests | Chunk |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| I1 | There is exactly one tenant-switch path. The core refuses a switch while a Harness decision is pending; the UI renders the archive-or-discard guard from the refusal and re-dispatches with the chosen resolution. | V2-R01 refusal on pending decision; V2-T01 guard rendered from refusal payload; V2-N01 switch via switcher, palette and menu all pass through the guard | C30 |\n| I2 | Every observed scope is per tenant. Switching tenants never shows another tenant's Inbox items, reads, reviews, conversations, drafts or rotation state. | V2-R02 scope isolation across two tenants; V2-T02 switcher round-trip leaves per-tenant transient state intact | C30 |\n| I3 | A review is bound to the tenant and resource it was opened for and applies only when the typed name matches, validated in the core. A tenant change invalidates the review. | V2-R03 confirmReview refuses on mismatch and on changed context; V2-T03 Apply disabled until names match | C31 |\n| I4 | Reads are finite. The UI distinguishes \"first read pending\" from \"no activity\", reports per-product partial coverage, and the completion toast is warn-kind whenever any source is partial. | V2-R04 ReadStatus transitions; V2-T04 toast kind and text per coverage; V2-N02 pending state visible on first run | C30 |\n| I5 | API responses are immutable snapshots. A later configuration change never alters an earlier response in history; a newly sent request reflects the change. | V2-R05 history entries hash-stable; V2-T05 topic applied → old response unchanged, new response includes it | C33 |\n| I6 | An issued secret is disclosed only through the host (`RevealSecret` sheet, host clipboard), is held per result for the active context and never persisted; any review exit, including a tenant switch or context retirement, drops it (`ForgetSecret`) and resets the reveal state. A dropped secret is not recoverable; the UI offers to issue another key. | V2-R06 held key dropped on review exit, switch and retirement; Reveal after drop refuses; V2-T06 resumed rotation shows no secret controls | C31 |\n| I7 | Key rotation is the admitted sequence additional key (`Version`) → local deployment acknowledgement → old key `Delete` with readback. Until the old key is removed, the reminder is a distinct Inbox item with its own identity and stated return time and the key table shows both keys. Two timestamps are stored once and labelled distinctly: deployment acknowledged (local statement) and old key removed (remote readback). A failed or unknown issuance never reissues automatically; a rejected or unknown Delete keeps the reminder and leaves the completion timestamp unset; unknown outcomes are shown as unconfirmed with reconciliation, never as complete. | V2-R07 state machine incl. failed/unknown issuance and rejected/unknown delete; V2-T07 Inbox item lifecycle; V2-T08 timestamps labelled and stable after refresh | C31 |\n| I8 | One topmost modal. Siblings of the topmost dialog are `aria-hidden`; Escape closes only the topmost; focus returns to the opener. | V2-T09 stacking; V2-N03 keyboard-only dialog traversal | C28 |\n| I9 | A verdict or severity is colour plus icon plus word, never colour alone. | V2-T10 every verdict renderer emits all three; V2-N04 accessibility tree carries the word | C28 |\n| I10 | Toasts have a kind (ok, info, warn), expire on a timer, and are presentation only: everything a toast says is derivable from an observed scope. With Reduce Motion on, toasts persist until dismissed. | V2-T11 kinds and expiry; V2-N05 Reduce Motion persistence | C28 |\n| I11 | Inbox acknowledge and snooze are per item, core-owned, with the return time stated wherever the action is offered (Home, Inbox row, Inbox detail) and identical across them. | V2-R08 snooze return computed in core; V2-T12 Home and Inbox labels equal | C30 |\n| I12 | A fresh tenant never shows seeded or sample data. Its first Harness send creates a user conversation; its Inbox is empty until the first read lands. | V2-R09 fresh tenant scopes empty; V2-T13 first send creates conversation | C30, C32 |\n\nRules I1, I3, I6 and I7 are authority rules under D014: the core owns the\ndecision, the UI renders it. No invariant may be satisfied by invented data: a\nproduct, tab or operation without an admitted service renders as unavailable\n(DESIGN-V2 §6), and a test that passes only on fixtures for such a capability\nis a specification defect.\n# Provenance and intentional deviations\n\nEvery fixture file and screen contract cites the frozen prototype file and the\nprototype state names it derives from. This file lists where the\nimplementation deliberately differs from the prototype and why. Anything not\nlisted here is expected to match the prototype's behaviour and spacing.\n\nFrozen reference: canvas https://claude.ai/artifact/2SEXT1XMHdZkxHvdw8oaYL,\npage \"Refined Native v2\", version 134 (version id 1793059808-4929, published\n2026-10-26). Files and hashes: [prototype/MANIFEST.sha256](prototype/MANIFEST.sha256).\n\n| # | Prototype | Implementation | Reason |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| P1 | Wizard tenant step draws the scan key, management client secret and Gateway workspace key as text fields (`R2Onboard` `f.scanKey`, `f.secret`, `f.gwKey`) | \"Add scan key…\" button opens the host's native secret sheet; the step then shows a masked indicator and the vault record id | The web layer never holds a secret (D014, C09 reveal ownership). Visual delta: button plus masked state instead of a field |\n| P2 | Rotation panel renders the secret in a readonly input with Reveal/Copy (`R2App` `secretShown`) | Reveal shows the secret in the host secret sheet; Copy goes through the host clipboard; both consume a one-time disclosure token | Invariant I6; same reason as P1 |\n| P3 | State lives in one component with `byTenant` (`R2App` `TS/ts/tPatch`) | Core-owned scopes per [state-ownership.md](state-ownership.md) | D014 authority; prototype state model is normative for invariants only |\n| P4 | ⌘1–⌘9 are advertised but not bound (canvas rule: no global key handlers) | Native menu defines the shortcuts and dispatches `navigate` | Host owns menus; works under VoiceOver and shows in the menu bar |\n| P5 | Sidebar lists Home, Inbox, Harness, Academy, Test Cases, API Client, Administration | Same, backed by Rust feature ids `home`, `inbox`, `harness`, `academy`, `library`, `apiClient`, `administration`; `gateway` and `redTeam` move inside Administration; `sessions`, `import`, `settings` reachable from the palette and Administration | One Rust inventory feeds menu, sidebar and palette |\n| P6 | Wizard boards are 1440×900 with a fixed 320px rail | Minimum window 1180×720; rail collapses to icons below 1280 wide | 13-inch displays |\n| P7 | Toasts expire on timers always | Persist until dismissed under Reduce Motion | Native acceptance Reduce Motion check |\n| P8 | Inbox items, Harness lifecycles, API responses, cases and Academy progress are invented in-component data | Wire fixtures in `packages/desktop-contracts/fixtures/wire`, content derived from the prototype data | Fixtures are the contract test surface; prototype data is never shipped |\n| P9 | `TOPICS.injo` added in round 16 as a separate key from detector `inj` | Core topic inventory keys are a distinct type from detector ids | Carries the round-16 fix into the type system |\n| P10 | Administration creation label is a per-product single value | Per-tab creation capability and label in `AdminInventory` | Round 16 finding: wrong label on API keys, Security profiles and Logs tabs |\n| P11 | Home snooze button hard-codes \"Snooze 24 hours\" | Item-specific `snoozeLabel` shared by Home and Inbox | Round 16 finding; invariant I11 |\n| P12 | API Client Send has no live completion status | Compact `role=\"status\"` region with request name, response code and duration; JSON body outside the live region | Round 16 finding |\n| P13 | Tenant switch with an open applied key review leaves disclosure open | Any review exit, including switch, finalises disclosure | Round 16 finding; invariant I6 |\n| P14 | Applied injection-override examples show \"Not demonstrated\" in the Playground | Topic examples classify under the topic key with a profile-aware verdict | Round 16 finding |\n| P15 | Import from v1 is a separate destination | Also offered on the wizard tenant step as a secondary action; credentials re-entered via the secret sheet | First run is where an upgrading user needs it |\n| P16 | Harness lifecycle 2 (mcp) reads an AES endpoint inventory (`CONVOS.mcp.tool` = `aes-inventory/get_endpoints`) and its follow-up is an AES policy review | The fixture reads the workspace MCP server inventory the core already exposes (`GatewayRead::McpServers`, C22) and the follow-up opens a Gateway guardrail review (`ChangeKind::GatewayGuardrails`) | AES has no admitted service (P18); the lifecycle stays read-only as in the prototype |\n| P17 | Wizard tenant step requires a management OAuth client id and secret (`ERR.clientId`, `ERR.secret`) and makes the scan key optional (`scanOn`) | First run requires the scan key and makes the management client an optional second section; add mode keeps both optional. Secrets enter only through the host secret sheet | D013 (scan-key-first; management credentials are separate and never imply scan authentication) |\n| P18 | Six products are verified, inventoried and administered (AIS tool policies, AES discovered AI and policies, AES policy review `REVIEWS.aes`) | Only products with an admitted service are verified, inventoried or changed: Runtime Security (`ManagementResource`, `ChangeKind`), Gateway (`GatewayRead`, Gateway `ChangeKind`s), Red Team and Model/skill reads (C13, read-only). Agentic Identity Security and Agentic Endpoint Security render as \"Not available in this release\" with their dependency gate; no inventory, verification result or mutation is invented for them | The existing interfaces do not supply those product APIs; fixtures cannot establish a capability (parity matrix, C13 rule) |\n| P19 | Wizard Academy step creates a local account with streaks, reminders and a quiz (`s.acad`, `s.af`, `s.streaks`, `s.remind`, `s.quiz`) | The step signs in to the hosted Academy identity or skips; the embedded learner view owns progress and gamification | D004–D006, C04 |\n| P20 | Key rotation issues a new key with a 24-hour grace period, a later revocation and a secret recoverable from the Keychain (`REVIEWS.key`, `keyRotated/keySecretClosed/keyDeployed/keyDoneAt`) | Two admitted operations in sequence: `ApiKeys · Version` (additional key, one-time reveal through the existing `RevealSecret` host sheet, never persisted) → local deployment acknowledgement → `ApiKeys · Delete` of the old key with readback. Two distinct timestamps; a lost secret means \"issue another key\" | `changes.rs` 842–846 and `management.rs` 980–988 define Rotate (regenerate, no grace) and Version (additional key); DESIGN.md \"Secrets\" forbids persisting issued keys |\n| P21 | Test Cases \"Run\" was described in the first spec draft as scanning any case | Runtime-only \"Open in Playground\"; \"Inspect\" for every other product, which never dispatches a scan | Prototype lines 1035–1037 and the parity matrix (no invented execution) |\n| P22 | Red Team Targets tab lists target names (`rtm` tables) | Targets shows `target_id` and `target_type` only | The C13 contract (`red_team.rs` 5–8, 56–66) deliberately keeps target names out of the core's reads; the privacy boundary is not widened for presentation |\n| P23 | One rotation flow for every key | Staged Version → Delete rotation for Runtime API keys only; Gateway keys use `Rotate` (regenerate in place) | `gateway_changes::allowed` admits Rotate, not Version, for Gateway keys |\n| P24 | Minimum window 1180×720 (DESIGN-V2 §4, tokens.md) | The host keeps `min_inner_size(900, 600)` until C34 because C01-N09 (re-run cumulatively by C24-N06) pins the legacy minimum; the v2 shell is laid out to stay usable without horizontal scroll at 900×600 (rail collapsed to icons below 1280 CSS px) and C28-N07 asserts it at that size. C34 raises the minimum together with the legacy-script retirement | Legacy native acceptance must keep passing while both shells exist (D058 §5) |\n| P25 | Revision of P1/P2: \"secrets enter only through a host secret sheet\" | The retained, reviewed entry path (C02/C09) is a webview password field read through a ref, cleared immediately and sent once inside the intent as `EnteredSecret` (zeroized, never in React state, never logged); the host owns the *reveal* sheet (`RevealSecret`) and the clipboard. C29 uses exactly this path for the scan key, management secret and Gateway key; no new host entry sheet is built | The C27 spec assumed an entry sheet the host does not have; the reviewed pattern already satisfies D014 and the C24 canary checks (no secret in any AX tree or file) |\n| P26 | `verifyTenantSetup` as one core intent streaming `VerifyStep`s (wizard.md) | Implemented as specified: `verifyTenantSetup{context}` runs in the core over the draft, admits the reads it needs through the management pipeline (management verify; the chosen Gateway workspace's prerequisites, or only the listing when none is chosen, reported as incomplete coverage; Red Team; model/skill) and settles each line from the result; AIS/AES are listed as not available and never read; the lines, `done`, `failed`, `blocking` and `stale` stream through `ShellSnapshot.setup.verify`. The scan key has no admitted read: its line is `stored`, never `pass`. Only the scan key (first run) blocks; an optional service's failure is carried as a warning the operator may continue over | Attempt-01 composed the reads in the presentation; attempt-02 read only the workspace listing and blocked on every failure — both rejected |\n| P27 | Add-tenant Cancel writes nothing except an already-vaulted credential | The wizard works on a core-owned draft (`beginTenantSetup`) with its own context and its own Gateway listing and choice; credentials attach to the draft; nothing is listed as a tenant (a draft id is refused by `selectTenant`/`deleteTenant`), the active tenant and its Gateway choice are untouched until `completeTenantSetup` commits the draft in one step and switches once. Every change to the draft's credentials or profile retires its context (and the binding, reads and listing under it). Cancel (`discardTenantSetup`) drops the draft, retires the binding and every read under its context and workspace reference, and queues its Keychain records for cleanup; the sheet closes only once the core admits it (a creation still pending or unconfirmed is awaited and whatever it produced is discarded). A relaunch, or a tenant committed outside the wizard during first run, does the same | Stronger than the spec's leftover credential: no orphan secrets and no cached authority surviving a replaced credential |\n| P28 | Gateway workspace key entered on the wizard tenant step | Implemented: an optional section reads the workspaces through the management client (`management` gateway inventory on the draft context), `selectGatewayWorkspace` chooses one, and `configureInferenceCredential` saves the endpoint and key for it | The admitted sequence (list → select → key) runs on the draft like on a tenant |\n| P29 | \"Import from v1\" fills the tenant step in place | The action navigates to the reviewed import destination (C23), which renders on its own during first run with a 'Back to setup' control; the wizard resumes afterwards | The import review flow is itself reviewed and must not be duplicated inside the wizard |\n| P30 | The wizard opens whenever onboarding is not done | The shell shows onboarding as done whenever a tenant already exists (an upgrade, or a tenant created before the v2 shell was chosen); the stored state is untouched. A first run is one with no tenant yet; a relaunch mid-setup reopens at the Tenant step with a fresh draft (the previous draft's credentials are cleaned up) | Existing users must never be sent through the wizard; a half-made draft is never a tenant |\n| P31 | Products step shows licence state read from the tenant | No admitted service reports licensing (DESIGN-V2 §6); each card shows what it reads with (scan key / management client, saved or not) and, once verified, that product's verify result; a note says licence state is not readable through the public APIs | Nothing may be inferred about licensing from reads that do not report it |\n| P32 | First-run import returns to the wizard | A v1 import during first run commits its tenants through the reviewed C23 flow; the desktop then has a tenant, so the first-run draft ends (its credentials are cleaned up) and the shell opens on the imported tenant; its scan key is added in Administration (the reviewed C08/C09 credential setup) | A tenant that exists is never sent back through a first-run wizard (P30), and the wizard cannot wrap an existing tenant without duplicating it |\n| P33 | `ReadStatus` as its own core scope with per-product states from dedicated reads | `readTenant` admits one finite read per chosen product through the existing management pipeline: the Runtime overview the shell already owns (sessions chart, key inventory, the chosen Gateway workspace's usage and limits), the Red Team statistics and scan list (C13's read), the model scans, skill statistics and skill scans (C13's read); each settles into the tenant's read record as a per-source outcome (read, needs a role, failed); a source counts as read only when it established what it is for: a listing whose page is the inventory (not capped, nothing beyond the first page), a sessions chart with consistent totals, a key page with every expiration reported, Gateway usage with the day's requests and errors both known; anything less is named in `sourcesMissing` (the same gaps the Inbox shows). A product attempt that established nothing (every source failed, or the read never reached the service) is kept as the latest failed attempt beside the product's last outcome that did, which stays shown as older; the read is failed while such an attempt is the latest (a missing role is a settled attempt reported on the product, not a failed read). Once anything was attempted, every chosen source nothing has reached yet is named as unread coverage too, and `settledAt` carries the time of the latest attempt so a completion that established nothing is announced for when it settled, never as a first read still pending. A healthy empty read older than the ten-minute window (`OverviewView.fresh`, C12) is a gap: Home and the Inbox keep the cached read and name its age instead of an all-clear, never re-reading on their own; a refresh in progress keeps that read and its age named until the new read lands (admission restores nothing). A chosen Gateway with no workspace is unread coverage, named in `sourcesMissing`; `recheckProduct` re-reads one chosen product. The read completes (one generation) when every part admitted under the tenant's current context has landed; a read in which no source answered is a failed read (`failed`, with `failedAt`), never pending and never done, and a