{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1261,1740p' docs/planning/decisions.md\"","aggregated_output":"  the errors and no longer reports it: a read of another workspace, or an\n  unknown day, keeps it. An overview whose Runtime sources failed but whose\n  Gateway answered is kept.\n- **Work kept on this Mac (T23).** The tenant's Gateway book also keeps:\n  - one Playground draft per workspace (model, route, system text and\n    prompt; saved as typed, within the run bounds);\n  - the record of every run that was sent and did not complete (stopped,\n    or failed after it was sent): workspace, model, route, trace, how it\n    ended, its outcome and token counts, never its output.\n  Both are listed under \"Kept on this Mac\" (offline too). A kept draft is\n  edited there by its local ID (`SavePlaygroundDraft` with the draft's\n  `id` and no workspace), which needs no listing or workspace reference\n  and so works offline. A pending edit is kept when the form loses focus\n  or closes, not only after the pause in typing. Nothing is replayed on\n  relaunch or reconnect, and a run is always an explicit press from the\n  chosen workspace with its stored key.\n- **Not done here.** File operations beyond D045's subset, the model catalogue\n  (`/models`), log exports, audit logs, pricing, limit-policy writes and\n  entity resets are not provided. Spoken VoiceOver output is not recorded.\n  No real endpoint is called before C25, and no live inference budget is\n  used.\n\n## D045 — Playground APIs and Gateway files: chat completions, Responses and a bounded file subset\n\nDecided in C18 remediation (implementer decision for the C18 milestone\n\"supported response/file workflows\", recorded for review). Corrected after\nreview attempt 2: an earlier version of this decision said the pinned SDK\ncould not upload files. That was wrong. `RuntimeClient::create_file`\n(`scm-sdk-0.1.0/src/airs/gateway/runtime_audio.rs`, multipart `POST /files`,\ncompiled under the `gateway` feature this build already enables) uploads a\nfile, so the file requirement is met here with a bounded subset.\n\nThe pinned SDK's `RuntimeClient` and v1 were compared operation by\noperation. v1's Playground streamed chat completions only\n(`bridge/src/gateway-stream.ts`, `createChatCompletion`). It had no\nResponses or file workflow.\n\n| Operation (pinned `scm-sdk 0.1.0`, `RuntimeClient`) | v1 | C18 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `create_chat_completion_stream` | Playground | Supported (v1 parity). |\n| `create_response_stream` | None | Supported: a Playground run with the Responses API, optionally attaching one listed file. |\n| `create_response`, `create_completion(_stream)`, prompt completions/renders, embeddings | None | Not offered: the Playground streams, and v1 had none of them. |\n| `get_response`, `list_input_items`, `delete_response` | None | Not offered: Responses runs are sent with `store: false`, so the Gateway keeps no response to read back, continue or delete. |\n| `list_files` | None | Supported: the chosen workspace's files (metadata only; the first 200, more reported). An upload adds its file at the top and keeps the bound: a file pushed past it is reported as more. A creation time no date can render is shown as unknown, not refused. |\n| `create_file` (multipart) | None | Supported: one file chosen in the native sheet, at most 4 MiB, purpose `assistants`. |\n| `delete_file` | None | Supported: one listed file, after an in-app confirmation. The answer counts only if it names that file: `deleted: true` completes, `deleted: false` is a refusal, and an answer naming another file is uncertain (kept with its trace, the file still listed, never resent). |\n| `retrieve_file` | None | Not needed: the list already gives each file's metadata, and the upload's answer gives the new file's. |\n| `download_file` (content) | None | Not offered: the app never reads a file's content back (retention below). |\n| Purposes `batch`, `fine-tune`, `vision` | None | Not offered: this app has no batch, fine-tuning or image workflow to use them. |\n| Vector stores and their files, batches, fine-tuning jobs | None | Not offered: no v1 workflow and none in the plan. |\n\n- **Responses runs.** These follow D044's rules for runs: the same key,\n  endpoint, workspace binding, trace and route headers, bounds, single\n  send, outcomes and records. The request is `input` (the prompt) with the\n  system text as `instructions`, `max_output_tokens` 256, `stream: true`\n  and `store: false`. With a file attached, `input` is one user message of\n  an `input_text` (the prompt) and an `input_file` (the file's ID). The SDK\n  ends the stream at the completed, incomplete or failed event. Text and\n  refusal deltas are output. The completed or incomplete event gives the\n  response's ID, served model, token counts and end. A failed or error\n  event ends the run as failed, named `modelFailed`; its outcome is\n  completed, since the Gateway answered. Tool, audio and search events are\n  not asked for and add no output.\n- **Cut short (both APIs).** A Responses `response.incomplete`, or a chat\n  finish of `length` or `content_filter`, ends the run as `incomplete`:\n  the Gateway answered, so the outcome is completed and nothing is retried\n  or kept as uncertain. The status says the answer is incomplete and why\n  (the sanitized reason, for example `max_output_tokens`), and the partial\n  output, tokens and trace stay shown. It is never shown as \"Completed.\"\n- **Choice.** The Playground offers the API as a choice (Chat completions\n  by default). It is kept with the draft, shown with the run and kept in\n  an uncertain run's record.\n- **Files: binding.** Every file request uses the workspace inference key\n  and goes only to the runtime endpoint stored with it, admitted through\n  the same check as a run (current context, current workspace reference,\n  the key stored for that workspace). One file request runs at a time, in\n  the reference's operations domain, so another workspace or context stops\n  it. Replacing or removing the key withdraws the files view at once and\n  drops a chosen file. Requests carry a fresh trace header and no route.\n- **Files: choosing and uploading.** A file is chosen in the native sheet.\n  Rust reads it once, up to 4 MiB (a larger or empty file is refused with\n  its reason before anything is sent), and holds it in memory under a\n  random token. The presentation sees only its name and size, never its\n  path or bytes. One upload takes the chosen file; uploading again needs a\n  new choice. The multipart name drops the characters a part name may not\n  carry.\n- **Files: outcomes.** Each request is sent once and never retried. A\n  failed request keeps the SDK's finding (`remote_outcome`): a refusal (4xx)\n  changed nothing; a 5xx, a lost answer, or an answer that is not a file\n  may have stored or deleted it. An upload or delete with an unknown result\n  is kept on this Mac (tenant book, D044) with its action, file name, size,\n  file ID (for a delete), trace and failure. It is never sent again and\n  is shown offline until dismissed; reading the files shows what exists.\n- **Files: attachment.** A Responses run may attach one file, and only one\n  that the files last read for that workspace with that key listed. The\n  Playground offers those files; a draft keeps the chosen file's ID, and a\n  run with a file no longer listed (another key, another workspace, not\n  read since a relaunch) is refused before anything is sent.\n- **Files: retention.** An uploaded file stays in the Gateway until it is\n  deleted, here or elsewhere. The app never deletes one by itself, keeps no\n  copy of its content (not in the tenant book, logs or snapshots) and never\n  downloads one. Deleting asks first and names the file and workspace.\n\n## D046 — The native Harness engine: sealed resource, lifecycle and the C19 conversation\n\nDecided in C19 (implementer decision for the C19 milestones, recorded for\nreview).\n\n**The engine and its seal.** The engine is the pinned build of\n`prisma-airs-harness` 9331a58 (protocol `airs-desktop/1`, runtime\n0.1.7-desktop.2). It is not rebuilt here. It is the binary v1 sealed at v1\ncommit 74754c02: `packaging/harness/darwin-arm64.tar.gz`, a Git LFS object\nwith sha256 e79f564a…. That binary's SHA-256 (f8e49cee…) matches its\n`BUILD-INFO.json`, which names the source commit 9331a58 and the build\ncommand, and v1's runtime pin.\n\n`tools/harness/seal.py` reseals it for v2 and takes only the engine and\nits notices:\n- `airs-harness`;\n- `BUILD-INFO.json`, `DEPENDENCIES.json`, `LICENSE` and `NOTICE`;\n- `licenses/engine/`, the engine's dependencies' licenses.\n\nNode, the launcher scripts, the managed CLI and its `node_modules` are\nleft out. The output is deterministic. It consists of:\n- `resources/harness/darwin-arm64.tar.gz` (Git LFS, 113 MB): the files\n  above and a `MANIFEST.json` inventory;\n- `resources/harness/pin.json`: the pin record, compiled into\n  desktop-core.\n\n`xtask native-prepare` extracts the archive and checks every file against\nthe inventory and the pin. A Tauri overlay of its own,\n`tauri.harness.conf.json`, bundles it as `Contents/Resources/harness/`; the\nfixture overlay still only renames the identity. Signed release packaging\nis C26.\n\n**Verification on every start.** Before a conversation reads its key or\nlaunches anything, the bundled executable must be:\n- an absolute path named `airs-harness`;\n- a regular file, not a link;\n- unchanged while it is read (same device, inode, size and time);\n- the pinned SHA-256.\n\nA changed or missing engine fails as `engineUnavailable`. Nothing is\nstarted and no key is read. The check is part of the measured start\n(`harness-start-ready`, C19 workload).\n\n**Lifecycle (DESIGN §3).** desktop-core owns the conversation policy.\ndesktop-host owns the OS adapter: `sidecar::OsLauncher` spawns, owns the\nprocess group and reaps. The engine starts with:\n- no inherited environment;\n- the argument `desktop-server` only;\n- stdin and stdout piped, and stderr discarded, so raw engine diagnostics\n  never reach a view or a log;\n- its own process group;\n- a fresh `0700` runtime home, `<app data>/harness-runtime/run-<hex>`, as\n  its `AIRS_HARNESS_HOME`.\n\nThe environment it gets is exactly:\n- `PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin` and `LANG`;\n- `TMPDIR`, inside the home;\n- `AIRS_HARNESS_HOME`;\n- `CODEX_CA_CERTIFICATE`: a file in the home holding the app transport\n  policy's additional roots, only when there are any;\n- the transport policy's proxy mode: `NO_PROXY=*` for Direct, the\n  explicit proxy URL (never one with credentials), or nothing for\n  System.\n\nThe key reaches the engine only in the bootstrap frame on stdin, encoded\ninto one zeroizing buffer within the 64 KiB frame bound.\n\nStopping closes stdin, waits 2 s, kills the process group, waits 2 s, and\nreaps. Exit is observed without reaping (`waitid` with `WNOWAIT`), so the\ngroup is killed while it is still the engine's, and nothing is signalled\nafter reaping. The engine runs a supervisor process of its own, which\ngoes with the group. A process that cannot be reaped is\n`cleanupFailed`, never success. The home is then removed; at a start,\nhomes left by an earlier run are removed too.\n\n**Binding and retirement.** A conversation is admitted exactly as a\nPlayground run is (D044):\n- the current context;\n- the chosen Gateway workspace's reference;\n- the inference key stored for that workspace, sent only to the runtime\n  endpoint stored with it.\n\nThe engine's identifiers are UUIDs. The workspace's Gateway ID is one.\nThe tenant and credential IDs are derived UUIDs (SHA-256 of a purpose and\nthe native ID, shaped as version 8). The credential epoch is 1 until C20\nbrings refresh epochs. The receipt must echo the binding exactly, with no\nMCP server.\n\nA conversation is its own task, not one of the four operation slots,\nbecause it may last hours (at most eight). It is retired the moment it is\nno longer bound, which every published change checks: another context,\nanother workspace, or the key replaced or removed. Its view is withdrawn\nat once, and its engine is stopped and reaped. Ending it, sleep\n(NSWorkspace will-sleep) and quitting also stop it; quitting waits up to\n5 s for the reap.\n\n**The C19 conversation.** C19 runs a read-only conversation:\n- no project and no local environment;\n- `dynamicTools` empty;\n- approval policy on-request.\n\nEvery approval or elicitation the engine asks for is declined, and any\nother engine request is refused. Tools, project access and approvals are\nC21; workspace MCP servers are C22.\n\nOne prompt runs at a time: at most 32 KiB, as eight 8 KiB text items.\nAnswers stream into a memory-only transcript:\n- at most 200 entries and 1 MiB of text;\n- one answer at most 256 KiB; past that, the answer is stopped and marked\n  truncated;\n- any copy of the key in engine text is replaced.\n\nStop answer sends `turn/interrupt`. Nothing of a conversation is written\nto disk. Protected history and restore are C20.\n\n**Retries.** The pinned engine's provider retries a Gateway 5xx or\ntransport failure four more times within one attempt. It also reconnects\na dropped or failed stream up to five times, each with those retries\nagain. A failing Gateway was measured to receive 30 requests for one\nmessage. The bootstrap cannot change this. Instead, the host stops the turn\nat the engine's first reconnect notice (an `error` notification with\n`willRetry`), and the turn ends as failed. A failing Gateway therefore sees\nat most the five requests of one attempt, and no 4xx is retried. The\nengine's in-attempt retries remain an engine property. Removing them\nneeds an engine change. Live qualification is C25.\n\n**Not done here.** The following are not done in C19:\n- Gateway sign-in and refresh (C20);\n- protected history and restore (C20);\n- tools, proposals and project access (C21);\n- MCP (C22);\n- importing v1 history (C23);\n- signed packaging (C26).\n\n## D047 — Harness company sign-in, renewal and protected history\n\nDecided in C20 (implementer decision for the C20 milestones, recorded for\nreview).\n\n**Credential choice.** A tenant may configure one company sign-in for the\nHarness: the Gateway workspace and runtime endpoint the token is for, and\nthe identity provider's issuer, public client ID and Gateway audience. The\noperator chooses, for that workspace, whether new conversations answer with\nthe workspace inference key (C18) or the company sign-in; signing in chooses\nthe sign-in. This follows v1's choice of a selected SSO session, otherwise\nthe stored key. Changing the choice, signing out, removing the\nconfiguration or the tenant retires the running conversation. The engine\nsends either credential as the Gateway key (`x-portkey-api-key`), as v1 did.\n\n**Sign-in.** OpenID Connect authorization code with PKCE (S256) through the\nsystem browser and a loopback redirect on `127.0.0.1` at an ephemeral port\n(RFC 8252 §7.3). The app is a public client.\n- Discovery must name exactly the configured issuer. It must support the\n  code flow and S256. Every endpoint (authorization, token, JWKS,\n  revocation) must be HTTPS on the issuer's own origin, so metadata never\n  grants a new destination (the Academy rule, D028). `offline_access` is\n  asked for when listed.\n- State and nonce are 256-bit random values. The verifier is 384 bits.\n- The listener lives for one attempt: 5 minutes, at most 32 connections,\n  3 s per connection, 8 KiB per request and 4 KiB per code.\n  - Only `GET /callback` with exactly one `Host` naming the listener is\n    considered.\n  - Only this attempt's `state` (compared in constant time) ends it.\n    Anything else is answered and ignored, so another local page can\n    neither complete nor cancel the attempt.\n  - An `iss` parameter must be the issuer. It is required when the provider\n    says it sends one (RFC 9207).\n- Replies are bounded at 128 KiB (JWKS 64 KiB, 32 keys), redirects are\n  refused, and 5xx or transport failures read as network failures.\n\n**Verification (stricter than v1, which did not check signatures).**\n- Both the ID token and the Gateway access token are JWTs, verified with\n  `jsonwebtoken` 11.1.0's verified `decode` against the issuer's JWKS. The\n  decode is never unchecked.\n- Only asymmetric algorithms are accepted: RS256/384/512, PS256/384/512,\n  ES256, ES384 and EdDSA. `none` and HMAC never verify.\n- The key must match the header: by `kid`, signature use, declared\n  algorithm, type and curve, and an RSA modulus of at least 2048 bits.\n- An unknown `kid` allows one bounded JWKS refresh per verification, so\n  each later key rotation is followed too (P06). Failing that, the token\n  is refused, never accepted unsigned.\n- ID token: issuer; the client as audience; the authorized party when\n  there are several audiences, or when present; this attempt's nonce; and\n  expiry and issued-at with 30 s skew.\n- Access token: issuer; the configured audience; `azp` or `client_id` when\n  present must be this client; expiry, not-before and issued-at with 30 s\n  skew. A `portkey_workspace` claim, when present, must be the chosen\n  workspace's slug.\n- The access token's `sub` is the grant's account. After a grant exists,\n  a token for another subject is `differentAccount` and the new refresh\n  token is revoked.\n- Opaque access tokens are refused (`opaqueToken`): no introspection\n  contract exists.\n- The provider is `aws_lc_rs`, not the `rust_crypto` that qualification.md\n  proposed. Reasons:\n  - aws-lc-rs 1.18.2 is already in the shipped graph (rustls-webpki).\n  - `rust_crypto` would add `rsa` 0.9, which carries an advisory with no\n    fixed release (RUSTSEC-2023-0071, a timing side channel in private-key\n    operations). Verification does not use private keys, but this choice\n    keeps the crate out.\n  - Default features are off: no PEM.\n- `openidconnect`/`oauth2` are not used, for D028's reasons: the authority\n  owns cancellation, flow supersession and revocation of a grant that\n  lands after cancel. Their HTTP integration targets another reqwest.\n  The flow is a small, fully bounded request set over the app's own client.\n\n**Grants and epochs.** Signing in creates a grant: a random UUID kept with\nthe configuration (nonsecret). The engine binding's credential is\n`derived_uuid(\"sign-in-grant\", grant)` with epoch 1. For the workspace key\nit stays C19's `derived_uuid(\"credential\", record)`.\n- Renewals keep the grant, so the binding, and with it the engine's exact\n  `replay.context == context` check, is unchanged.\n- Signing in again to the same account under a live grant (after a refused\n  renewal) also keeps it.\n- Signing out, removing the configuration or the tenant ends the grant. A\n  later sign-in is a new grant: it never revives an old conversation, whose\n  saved history can then be read and exported but not continued.\n- Changing the configuration requires signing out first.\n- The refresh token is a Keychain item (JSON, one per grant, new record\n  per rotation) under the C02 write-ahead cleanup ledger. The access token\n  is kept in memory only.\n- Both writes (sign-in and rotation) follow the confirmed write-ahead\n  contract (P06, C20 review F2). The record joins the ledger, the save is\n  confirmed durable, and only then is it written. It is marked in flight\n  until the save that settles it, under that save's lock, so cleanup and\n  reset wait for it. An unconfirmed ledger writes nothing. A\n  write that lands after its sign-in was cancelled, signed out or\n  superseded stays in the ledger and is deleted; the new token is revoked.\n- One renewal runs at a time per grant and runs to completion even if its\n  caller goes away. A rotated token is saved or revoked, never lost.\n- A sign-in again to the same account renews the live grant in place under\n  the same lock (P06, C20 review F3). It waits for a renewal under way, and\n  a renewal waits for it. A rotation commits only if the stored record is\n  still the one it started from. Exactly the current refresh record stays\n  owned, and the grant (with it the engine binding) is unchanged.\n\n**Renewal and restart.** Before each message, a sign-in conversation\ncompares the wall clock with the engine token's renewal time,\n`exp − min(120 s, lifetime/2)`.\n- Inside the margin, the message is held (phase `renewing`) while the\n  token is renewed. The engine accepts its credential only in the\n  bootstrap, so the running engine is then stopped and reaped. A new engine\n  is started with the new token and the same binding, and\n  `desktop/history/restore` restores the conversation's last export. Only\n  then is the message sent.\n- A refused or failed renewal sends nothing. The message is given back to\n  the box, and the conversation stays with `sessionExpired` (sign in\n  again).\n- A transient network failure gives the message back without expiring the\n  grant.\n- A conversation with turns but no export to restore cannot continue\n  (`restoreFailed`); it is never silently restarted without context.\n- An export matches the conversation only until its next turn ends. A\n  turn whose export is refused or unanswered leaves nothing to restore, so\n  the renewal fails as `restoreFailed` without starting an engine or\n  sending the message. An older export never stands in for newer turns\n  (P06, C20 review F1).\n- Sleep still ends conversations (C19), so expiry across sleep is met at\n  the next start or resume. The C20 conversation has no tools or effects,\n  so \"rollback to a pre-turn checkpoint\" has nothing to roll back: a\n  failed turn stays shown as failed, and nothing is resent. Tool and\n  effect recovery, and WorkspaceWrite re-confirmation on restart, are C21.\n\n**Protected history.** After every turn (completed, stopped, cut or\nfailed) the host asks the idle engine for `desktop/history/read`. A turn\ncut short when the engine itself is stopped is saved with the last export,\nor without one when none exists (`notRestorable`: read and exported, never\ncontinued), and its unfinished entries `interrupted` (D049, P06).\nA stop that comes after a turn finished, while its export is still under\nway, waits for it for up to 3 s. The turn is then saved with its export\nand stays continuable. A finished turn's transcript counts as unsaved\nuntil a save succeeds. If its export does not come, is refused or is\nmalformed, or never answers within its 15 s bound, a stop saves the\ntranscript without a restore point: read-only, and never paired with an\nolder export (P06 phase review F1, R2-F2). Once a stop has begun, nothing\noperator-sent is released, after any await: not a message waiting for an\nexport, not one still queued, and not a renewal's (no engine is started\nfor it). Each such message is kept in the transcript as `failed`, saved\nwith it (the last export stays the restore point for the turns it holds),\nand never sent or replayed later; so is a renewal's message whose new\nengine never runs (its home, launch or pipes fail, or the old home cannot\nbe removed), saved with the last export (P06 phase review R4-F1). A\nrenewal a stop overtakes while its\nsign-in is renewed, or while changes wait to be allowed again, gives its\nmessage back to the box instead (P06 phase review R2-F1, R3-F1, R3-F2).\nMessages wait for the read, which is bounded by the 15 s request timeout. The\nconversation is saved as one sealed record:\n- the transcript;\n- the binding it ran under;\n- the engine's replay and context exactly as exported.\n\nStorage:\n- `<data>/harness-history/<tenant digest>/<scope digest>/<id>.sealed`, one\n  scope per tenant and Gateway workspace.\n- XChaCha20-Poly1305 (`chacha20poly1305` 0.10.1, D031; the DESIGN's 0.11.0\n  is not adopted), with a fresh 24-byte nonce per write.\n- A random 32-byte key per scope in the Keychain, at a record derived from\n  the scope, made on first save.\n- Associated data binds the schema, the scope and the record ID.\n- Files are 0600 in 0700 directories and replaced atomically.\n- Scopes are noted in the state document before their first write, so\n  removing a tenant removes its keys (cleanup ledger) and its directory.\n- Each save is admitted under the authority lock (storage ready, tenant\n  present, the scope's note confirmed durable) and counted until it\n  settles. An unconfirmed note writes nothing (C20 review R2). Its scope key is kept in flight\n  meanwhile (P06, C20 review F4), and saves are refused once reset or\n  tenant removal begins. Tenant removal deletes the directory only after\n  that tenant's saves settle, and cleanup deletes the key after them.\n  Reset waits for every save, then purges the history folder with the\n  Keychain namespace; a failure is reported as a reset failure. A launch\n  removes history folders of tenants that no longer exist.\n- Locked, denied or missing storage never falls back to plaintext. The\n  conversation goes on unsaved and says why.\n\nLimits:\n- 16 conversations per workspace. A seventeenth is refused (`full`),\n  never made room for by deleting.\n- 8 MiB per record. The engine's replay is at most 4 MiB, and the\n  transcript bounds are C19's.\n- A record that does not open is listed as unreadable, can be deleted, and\n  is never rewritten.\n\nOperations:\n- **Open** reads one record.\n- **Export** writes the transcript only (Markdown, text or JSON), never the\n  replay or context, through the native save sheet.\n- **Delete** asks for confirmation (an unreadable record too: its key may\n  be missing only for now) and is refused for the running\n  conversation.\n- **Continue** starts a new engine and restores the record. It is offered\n  only when the record's binding equals the binding a new conversation\n  would have now (tenant, workspace, credential, epoch, endpoint and\n  credential choice). Otherwise the record is read-only and says why. The\n  engine checks the context again. The continued conversation keeps\n  updating the same record.\n- **Offline (T23).** With no workspace chosen, as when AI Gateway cannot\n  be read, the tenant's last chosen workspace's history is listed from\n  this Mac (`local`). It can be opened, exported and deleted but not\n  continued (`workspaceUnavailable`). A launch or reconnect never restarts\n  or resubmits a conversation; continuing is the operator's explicit\n  choice from history.\n- Imported v1 history is C23 and stays inspect-only.\n\n**Out of scope here:** tools, approvals, project access and WorkspaceWrite\n(C21); MCP and its token rotation (C22); v1 history import (C23); live\nidentity-provider and Gateway qualification (C25).\n\n## D048 — Harness desktop tools, project folder, approvals and proposals\n\nDecided in C21 (implementer decision for the C21 milestones, recorded for\nreview).\n\n**Desktop tools.** The engine is given one dynamic-tool namespace,\n`airs_desktop`, and nothing else of the desktop. Its tools are typed:\n- `inventory`, `detail`: the Runtime Security inventories C08 reads.\n  The DLP catalog is not offered.\n- `session`, `search`: C11's investigation reads. Session content is\n  already stripped by the management layer.\n- `redTeam`, `supplyChain`: C13's observation reads.\n- `scan`: one C03 scan with a profile named by the model, through the\n  tenant's own scan key.\n- `cases`: the C12 library's names and expectations.\n- `proposeChange`: stages a change for the operator to review (below).\n\nEvery call goes through the same admission as the operator's own reads:\n`admit_management`, `admit_scan` and the library. The call runs under the\nconversation's context and is refused once that context is stale. The\ndesktop holds the credentials. Results are JSON:\n- JSON carried as text is opened first: at most 4 layers deep and 4 MiB.\n  Text still encoded past those bounds is replaced by a note, never\n  passed on unchecked (P06, C21 reviews F2 and R2-F2);\n- field names that carry credentials are removed at any depth (14\n  names, case- and dash-insensitive);\n- the conversation's key is redacted;\n- a longer result becomes `{\"cut\": note, \"partial\": text}`, shortened\n  until the whole envelope, escaping included, fits 24 KiB (P06, C21\n  review R2-F4).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
